","nucleus_padj":5.03131e-65,"nucleus_log2fc":0.187872,"nucleus_score":17.0957,"pct_ad":0.395171,"pct_control":0.373113,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.568157,"donor_log2fc":0.219386,"donor_lfcse":0.0998412,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.0240736,"degas_log2fc":0.24518,"degas_padj":2.75493e-150},{"cell_type":"In","gene_symbol":"PPP1CC","gene_symbol_current":"PPP1CC","gene_id":"ENSG00000186298","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":true,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":0.0438157,"nucleus_log2fc":0.0465841,"nucleus_score":2.08558,"pct_ad":0.345461,"pct_control":0.343773,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.723592,"donor_log2fc":0.107961,"donor_lfcse":0.0638129,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.013668,"degas_log2fc":0.301403,"degas_padj":2.00262e-132},{"cell_type":"Mic","gene_symbol":"PPP1CC","gene_symbol_current":"PPP1CC","gene_id":"ENSG00000186298","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"lower in AD","nucleus_padj":0.393309,"nucleus_log2fc":-0.0550553,"nucleus_score":-1.0391,"pct_ad":0.155246,"pct_control":0.160829,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.678075,"donor_log2fc":-0.137397,"donor_lfcse":0.107205,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":-0.0243762,"degas_log2fc":-0.00115007,"degas_padj":0.989263},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_symbol":"PPP1CC","gene_symbol_current":"PPP1CC","gene_id":"ENSG00000186298","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":0.110812,"nucleus_log2fc":0.0887149,"nucleus_score":2.02393,"pct_ad":0.251566,"pct_control":0.234378,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.999342,"donor_log2fc":0.0140669,"donor_lfcse":0.0830535,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":2.15163e-06,"degas_log2fc":-0.142469,"degas_padj":0.0153035},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_symbol":"PPP1CC","gene_symbol_current":"PPP1CC","gene_id":"ENSG00000186298","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":true,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":1.13575e-11,"nucleus_log2fc":0.181384,"nucleus_score":6.94713,"pct_ad":0.198186,"pct_control":0.177504,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.455843,"donor_log2fc":0.189714,"donor_lfcse":0.0702865,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.117012,"degas_log2fc":-0.0688249,"degas_padj":0.0126637}],"modules":[{"cell_type":"Ast","gene_id":"ENSG00000186298","module":"magenta","kme":0.149965,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.456482,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Ex","gene_id":"ENSG00000186298","module":"red","kme":0.171287,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.0519952,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_id":"ENSG00000186298","module":"turquoise","kme":0.06961,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.842971,"module_significant":false}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000186298","entrez_id":["5501"],"uniprot":["P36873"],"ncbi_summary":"The protein encoded by this gene belongs to the protein phosphatase family, PP1 subfamily. PP1 is an ubiquitous serine/threonine phosphatase that regulates many cellular processes, including cell division. It is expressed in mammalian cells as three closely related isoforms, alpha, beta/delta and gamma, which have distinct localization patterns. This gene encodes the gamma isozyme. Alternatively spliced transcript variants encoding different isoforms have been found for this gene. [provided by RefSeq, Oct 2011].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"P36873","text":"Protein phosphatase that associates with over 200 regulatory proteins to form highly specific holoenzymes which dephosphorylate hundreds of biological targets (PubMed:17936702, PubMed:25012651). Protein phosphatase 1 (PP1) is essential for cell division, and participates in the regulation of glycogen metabolism, muscle contractility and protein synthesis. Dephosphorylates RPS6KB1 (PubMed:17936702). Involved in regulation of ionic conductances and long-term synaptic plasticity. May play an important role in dephosphorylating substrates such as the postsynaptic density-associated Ca(2+)/calmodulin dependent protein kinase II. Component of the PTW/PP1 phosphatase complex, which plays a role in the control of chromatin structure and cell cycle progression during the transition from mitosis into interphase (PubMed:20516061). In balance with CSNK1D and CSNK1E, determines the circadian period length, through the regulation of the speed and rhythmicity of PER1 and PER2 phosphorylation (PubMed:21712997). May dephosphorylate CSNK1D and CSNK1E (By similarity). Regulates the recruitment of the SKA complex to kinetochores (PubMed:28982702). Dephosphorylates the 'Ser-418' residue of FOXP3 in regulatory T-cells (Treg) from patients with rheumatoid arthritis, thereby inactivating FOXP3 and rendering Treg cells functionally defective (PubMed:23396208). Together with PPP1CA (PP1-alpha subunit), dephosphorylates IFIH1/MDA5 and RIG-I leading to their activation and a functional innate immune response (PubMed:23499489). Core component of the SHOC2-MRAS-PP1c (SMP) holophosphatase complex that regulates the MAPK pathway activation (PubMed:35768504, PubMed:35831509). The SMP complex specifically dephosphorylates the inhibitory phosphorylation at 'Ser-259' of RAF1 kinase, 'Ser-365' of BRAF kinase and 'Ser-214' of ARAF kinase, stimulating their kinase activities (PubMed:35768504, PubMed:35831509). Dephosphorylates MKI67 at the onset of anaphase (PubMed:25012651). The SMP complex enhances the dephosphorylation activity and substrate specificity of PP1c (PubMed:35768504, PubMed:35831509)."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_04","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}